Stop the user-space RFS server claiming to serve, and fix its re-root

The kernel driver answers RFS itself -- the S5XXX_CH_RFS arm of its rx path
hands every frame that fits one ring slot to an in-kernel server and sets
`port = NULL` -- so this server only ever sees an oversized frame, and none has
been seen. It nevertheless printed "rfs: serving on /dev/wwan0rfs0" and then sat
silent forever, which reads exactly like carrier config arriving here. It does
not arrive here, and believing it did cost a day of debugging a modem that was
in fact never asked for its carrier policy at all.

Keep the implementation, drop the claim. It is not dead code: the in-kernel
server cannot implement the write half -- drivers do not write files, so the
CP's NV writes are acked and discarded today -- and a mainline driver wants the
protocol in user space regardless, because carrier config is a rootfs tree that
the kernel's own documentation says only user space can know is ready
(Documentation/driver-api/firmware/direct-fs-lookup.rst). When the driver stops
answering, this is what answers.

Also carried here: the re-root the CP needs when it falls back to the built-in
Android path. There is no /vendor on a mainline rootfs, so a request for
/vendor/firmware/carrierconfig/... is tried against the directory the firmware
package actually installs before the bare staging one, instead of only the
latter.

Also folded in: the CP's NV writes are applied at the offset it names, over
the image we would serve, instead of landing in a file of their own. The CP
sends the region it changed -- the first 99 KiB of a 512 KiB nv_normal, at
offset 0 -- so truncating produced a 99 KiB file that is not a valid NV image,
and nothing it ever wrote could be given back to it. The seed source now
includes the directory the firmware package installs to, which is where the
pristine image actually lives on a mainline rootfs; /mnt/vendor/efs does not
exist there. With both, a write round-trips: 524288 bytes out, 524288 back.

